hare-lsp's overlay of stdlib hare::parse.

This patch is applied to a fresh copy of $(STDLIB)/hare/parse/parse.ha at
build time (see the Makefile's hare/parse/.stamp target). The materialized
copy is gitignored; only this patch and the README are tracked.

What it changes:
  - Adds a top-of-file comment marking the file as the hare-lsp overlay.
  - In want(), replaces the two mkloc(lexer) calls with tok.2 (the failing
    token's own location).

Why:
  mkloc(lexer) does a lex+unlex cycle, which leaves a token in the lexer's
  single-slot unlex buffer. The lex::unlex(lexer, tok) call further down
  then trips the "attempted to unlex more than one token" assertion and
  aborts the process. That assertion fires routinely on intermediate
  editing states (e.g. the user has just typed `let x =` and is still
  typing the right-hand side), so an unmodified stdlib parser crashes the
  LSP. Substituting tok.2 keeps the unlex slot clean, and because tokstr
  ignores the location field for the keyword/symbol tokens used here, the
  rendered error message is identical.

To refresh for a new Hare release:
  1. make clean && make
     If the patch still applies cleanly, you are done.
  2. Otherwise, copy $(STDLIB)/hare/parse/parse.ha into a scratch dir,
     re-apply the fix and the comments by hand, run
     `diff -u --label a/parse.ha --label b/parse.ha orig new > parse.ha.patch`,
     and commit the new patch.

Apply with: `patch -p1 -d hare/parse < hare/parse/parse.ha.patch`

--- a/parse.ha
+++ b/parse.ha
@@ -1,5 +1,10 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0
 // (c) Hare authors <https://harelang.org>
+//
+// hare-lsp overlay. This file is the stdlib hare::parse::parse.ha with the
+// `want()` fix applied; see hare/parse/parse.ha.patch for the canonical
+// source of the change. The rest of the directory is materialized from the
+// system stdlib at build time and is not tracked in git.

 use fmt;
 use hare::lex;
@@ -40,16 +45,25 @@

 	let buf = memio::dynamic();
 	defer io::close(&buf)!;
+	// hare-lsp patch: stdlib uses mkloc(lexer) for the per-alternative
+	// location and for syntaxerr below. mkloc does a lex+unlex cycle,
+	// which leaves a token in the unlex slot - so the lex::unlex(lexer,
+	// tok) below would then assert with "attempted to unlex more than
+	// one token", aborting the process on intermediate editing states.
+	// We use tok.2 (the failing token's location) instead. tokstr
+	// ignores the location field for the keyword/symbol tokens used
+	// here, so the rendered error is identical, and the unlex slot
+	// stays clean.
 	for (let i = 0z; i < len(want); i += 1) {
 		const tstr = if (want[i] == ltok::NAME) "name"
-			else lex::tokstr((want[i], void, mkloc(lexer)));
+			else lex::tokstr((want[i], void, tok.2));
 		fmt::fprintf(&buf, "'{}'", tstr)!;
 		if (i + 1 < len(want)) {
 			fmt::fprint(&buf, ", ")!;
 		};
 	};
 	lex::unlex(lexer, tok);
-	return syntaxerr(mkloc(lexer), "Unexpected '{}', was expecting {}",
+	return syntaxerr(tok.2, "Unexpected '{}', was expecting {}",
 		lex::tokstr(tok), memio::string(&buf)!);
 };
